  "query_preview": "guidance continues to show a  stout dry cold front pushing \ninto the region by Friday. This will cause dewpoints to fall into \nthe mid 30s to mid 40s. At the very least, cool down still remains \nlikely next weekend. Highs will be seasonably warm Friday in the \nupper 80s to low 90s, with lows in the low to mid 60s that morning \nand lows that night upper 40s to low 50s north of Interstate 20 (48-\n52F) to mid to upper 50s (53-57F) to the south. Seasonable and \npleasant fall temperatures will build areawide into next weekend. \n\nThe combination of continued drought stressed vegetation and \nlongstanding limited rainfall will help increase fire danger \nconcerns. Long term guidance continues to a 1035-1045mb sfc high \nbuilding east out of the lee side of the Rockies through the \nMidwest. This will lead to a 6-8mb pressure gradient and result in \ngusty gradient wind/wind gusts reaching 30-35 mph at times across \nour area on Friday. At this time, no changes have been made to the \nwind graphic for Friday and a \"Limited\" threat for gusty winds will \ncontinue to be advertised for our entire forecast area. The Storm \nPrediction Center is still highlighting a relatively rare (for our \narea) Marginal Outlook for fire conditions on Friday with the \nexpected dry cold frontal passage. We will continue to refine our \nmessaging for fire danger locally, but do expect more \"Elevated\" \nwording for Friday's fire danger after several lower end/\"Limited\" \ndays this week. Low chances for scattered showers (PoPs around 15%) \nwill be possible across portions of west MS, southwest MS, and \nportions of northeast LA heading into Monday and again on Tuesday.\n/CR/",
